  };

  [38;5;153mlogger.event(name, fields)[39m looks the name up, drops the call entirely if the event is unknown, keeps only declared
  fields, and takes the level from the catalog rather than the caller.

  The invariant that makes this worth anything, and which I'd write into the spec as a rule: [1mallowlisted fields carry [0m
  [1mdeveloper-controlled values — enums, codes, numbers — never a value read from an input element.[0m [38;5;153moutcome[39m is
  [38;5;153m"success"[39m/[38;5;153m"failure"[39m, not the server's response body. A field whose value comes from the user is not allowlistable.

  That rule has one consequence worth surfacing now: it excludes [38;5;153merror.message[39m, because thrown messages routinely
  interpolate user input. So [38;5;153munhandled_error[39m records the error's [3mname[0m and location, not its message. You lose some
  diagnostic detail; you gain the guarantee.

  [1mWire format[0m

  {
    [36m"schema"[39m: [32m1[39m,
    [36m"sessionId"[39m: [31m"018f..."[39m,
    [36m"events"[39m: [
      { [36m"ts"[39m: [31m"2026-09-16T18:22:01.412Z"[39m, [36m"level"[39m: [31m"info"[39m, [36m"event"[39m: [31m"login_attempt"[39m,
        [36m"fields"[39m: { [36m"outcome"[39m: [31m"failure"[39m, [36m"durationMs"[39m: [32m214[39m }, [36m"url"[39m: [31m"https://app/login"[39m }
    ],
    [36m"dropped"[39m: [32m0[39m
  }

  [38;5;153msessionId[39m is a [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m generated per page load and held [1min memory only[0m — no cookie, no [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. It
  correlates events within one session, and deliberately cannot track a person across sessions. [38;5;153mdropped[39m reports how many
  events the buffer discarded under pressure, so a gap in the logs is visible rather than silent.

  [38;5;153mschema: 1[39m is there because the collector doesn't exist yet; versioning the envelope from the first line costs nothing
  now and is the thing you'd most wish you had once a collector is parsing it.

  Does part 1 look right?

